    "interpretation": "A4000 grouped output matches standalone actual-width native NS exactly for widths 32/64/128/256 in these seeds; larger shapes can differ due to GEMM kernel rounding. Both BF16 paths differ by about 1% from the FP64 iteration initialized with identical rounded normalization. Numerical differences are reported, without choosing pass tolerances from observed errors."
  },
  "interpretation": "Actual-size grouped GEMM reduces isolated NS work, but full-step gains depend on hardware and zero skipping. On A4000, the baseline zero-skip optimization already removes much of the wasted work; grouped metadata/guard/copies and the unchanged first block/forward/backward limit the remaining gain. The breakdown measures preparation cost directly; it does not identify individual cuBLAS kernels because primary measurements intentionally omit profiling. Use the per-hardware paired one-step table for conclusions; do not mix runs or compare historical profiler times.",
